      <memberdef kind="function" id="namespaceCANProtocol_1aee035f550086e47e9c533b9f547687f9" prot="public" static="no" const="no" explicit="no" inline="no" virt="non-virtual">
        <type>void</type>
        <definition>void CANProtocol::serviceNodeStatus</definition>
        <argsstring>(NodeKind kind, uint16_t rxCount=0)</argsstring>
        <name>serviceNodeStatus</name>
        <qualifiedname>CANProtocol::serviceNodeStatus</qualifiedname>
        <param>
          <type><ref refid="NodeStatus_8h_1aac09d7d39a883b449ee56e0ab7f72cf4" kindref="member">NodeKind</ref></type>
          <declname>kind</declname>
        </param>
        <param>
          <type>uint16_t</type>
          <declname>rxCount</declname>
          <defval>0</defval>
        </param>
        <briefdescription>
<para>Run the node-generic status duties. Call once per loop(), after <ref refid="namespaceCANProtocol_1a3215742dd9da68b125f121c95425e2e4" kindref="member">drain()</ref>. </para>
        </briefdescription>
        <detaileddescription>
<para>Every node on the bus owes the same status traffic, whatever else it does (#340):<itemizedlist>
<listitem><para><computeroutput>HB_NODE_ID</computeroutput> every 500 ms (makeHeartbeatPayload), carrying <computeroutput>rxCount</computeroutput>.</para>
</listitem><listitem><para><computeroutput>HEALTH_NODE_ID</computeroutput> every 1000 ms (makeNodeHealthPayload) with <computeroutput>kind</computeroutput>, the internal die temp and the node fault, plus the DiagSerial fault edge log (tag <computeroutput>BRIDGE</computeroutput> on NODE_ID 0, <computeroutput>NODE</computeroutput> otherwise). Compiled out with <computeroutput>-DNODE_HEALTH_TELEM=0</computeroutput>.</para>
</listitem><listitem><para>Duplicate NODE_ID check (#344), on every node: a node never receives its own frames, so a frame on its own <computeroutput>HB_NODE_ID</computeroutput> (<ref refid="namespaceCANProtocol_1a88802da4205140719f9714cb416f1b2c" kindref="member">start()</ref> adds it to the filter) means another board has the same NODE_ID. The node fault is then <computeroutput><ref refid="NodeStatus_8h_1ad46bdfb956f3738768ac20fb36e8daf1a8ac276c7ffff11a1c7f7dcd869edea91" kindref="member">NodeFaultCode::DUPLICATE_NODE_ID</ref></computeroutput>, ahead of every FaultSource (the NODE_ID is wrong, so nothing else this ID reports can be trusted); it clears DUP_NODE_TIMEOUT_MS after the last foreign frame. aggregateFaults() supplies the fault otherwise.</para>
</listitem><listitem><para>Master-loss watchdog, on every node except <ref refid="namespacePanelBridge" kindref="compound">PanelBridge</ref> (NODE_ID 0): once a master heartbeat (<computeroutput>HB_0</computeroutput>) has been seen, master loss is set after MASTER_TIMEOUT_MS of silence and cleared by the next <computeroutput>HB_0</computeroutput>.</para>
</listitem><listitem><para>WARNING on a non-bridge node = master loss OR overheat (overheat only when a build defines <computeroutput>NODE_OVERHEAT_C</computeroutput>). The service tracks the two causes separately and writes the shared latch only when their OR changes, so a returning master never clears a live overheat, and overheat clears once the die cools. On <ref refid="namespacePanelBridge" kindref="compound">PanelBridge</ref> an overheat is raise-only, because its latch also carries <ref refid="namespacePanelBridge" kindref="compound">PanelBridge</ref>&apos;s dead-node WARNING.</para>
</listitem></itemizedlist>
</para>
<para>The first call arms the HB/HEALTH timers — the first loop() after setup(), i.e. boot complete — so the first HB goes out ~500 ms and the first HEALTH ~1000 ms after it.</para>
<para><computeroutput>HB_0</computeroutput> arrival is recorded by <ref refid="namespaceCANProtocol_1a3215742dd9da68b125f121c95425e2e4" kindref="member">drain()</ref>, which still passes the frame to <ref refid="namespaceCANProtocol_1ab9d0d6bb169c659d369e630d1630c0e1" kindref="member">onReceive()</ref>. <ref refid="namespaceCANProtocol_1a88802da4205140719f9714cb416f1b2c" kindref="member">start()</ref> adds <computeroutput>HB_0</computeroutput> to the hardware filter on non-bridge nodes, so callers never register it by hand.</para>
<para><parameterlist kind="param"><parameteritem>
<parameternamelist>
<parametername>kind</parametername>
</parameternamelist>
<parameterdescription>
<para>This node&apos;s board kind (#344), sent in every HEALTH_n. Each node library passes its own (<ref refid="namespacePanelGroup" kindref="compound">PanelGroup</ref> → PANEL_GROUP, <ref refid="namespacePduNode" kindref="compound">PduNode</ref> → PDU, <ref refid="namespacePanelBridge" kindref="compound">PanelBridge</ref> → BRIDGE), so a sketch never sets it. Required: a role can&apos;t forget it. </para>
</parameterdescription>
</parameteritem>
<parameteritem>
<parameternamelist>
<parametername>rxCount</parametername>
</parameternamelist>
<parameterdescription>
<para>Caller-owned accepted RX count for the heartbeat (<ref refid="namespacePanelGroup" kindref="compound">PanelGroup</ref>: accepted CTRL_BCAST frames). Pass 0 when the node keeps no count.</para>
</parameterdescription>
</parameteritem>
</parameterlist>
<simplesect kind="note"><para>Samples millis() itself, after <ref refid="namespaceCANProtocol_1a3215742dd9da68b125f121c95425e2e4" kindref="member">drain()</ref> has stamped any <computeroutput>HB_0</computeroutput>, and compares elapsed time signed — so a millisecond ticking during <ref refid="namespaceCANProtocol_1a3215742dd9da68b125f121c95425e2e4" kindref="member">drain()</ref> cannot read as a timeout (#225). </para>
</simplesect>
</para>
        </detaileddescription>
